WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.272
The famed Liu Song general Tan Daoji led his army north, but the army's grain was almost gone.

00:00:07.622 --> 00:00:14.702
Enemy spies heard the Song army was short of grain, crept close, and prepared to attack at the chance.

00:00:15.052 --> 00:00:19.348
Hidden in the dark, they quietly sent the news back to the enemy camp.

00:00:19.698 --> 00:00:27.738
Tan Daoji thought of a clever plan. He ordered the soldiers to measure sand at night, calling out the grain counts in loud voices.

00:00:28.088 --> 00:00:31.952
He was not panicked at all — he had long since thought of a plan.

00:00:32.302 --> 00:00:39.574
The soldiers spread a little white rice over the piles of sand, so from far away they looked like full stores of grain.

00:00:39.924 --> 00:00:48.108
The enemy saw from afar and believed the Song army had plenty of grain; frightened, they dared not attack and quietly withdrew.

00:00:48.458 --> 00:00:52.658
They retreated through the night and never dared pursue again.

00:00:53.008 --> 00:00:59.800
This is the famous story of 'singing the tallies while measuring sand' — Tan Daoji used wisdom to keep his army safe.

